course: dictionary
lesson: 2812
title: Distort: changing shape, sound and truth
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Distort means to change something so it is no longer true to its real shape, sound or meaning. Context gives you the clue: a fun-house mirror makes your face look twisted, so distort must mean changing the shape until it looks wrong.
explain: The word also works with facts. «Distort the truth» means changing what really happened so people get a false idea. Spotting this matters when you read news or adverts.
explain: Look at the words around distorts. In «It is impossible to flatten the surface of a sphere onto paper without stretching, squashing or tearing it, so every flat map distorts something», the clues are stretching, squashing and tearing. So distorts means changing the shape of the Earth's surface, and a flat map can never show it exactly.
fact: Distort means to change the shape, facts or sound of something.
fact: Distorted is the adjective, as in a distorted picture; distortion is the noun, as in the distortion on a guitar.
fact: Light passing through water or a lens can distort things, so a straw in a glass looks bent.
fact: A flat map must distort something, because a round Earth cannot be flattened without stretching, squashing or tearing it.
fact: When someone distorts the facts, they give a false idea, so always check where news comes from.
exercise: A fun-house mirror makes your face look wide and squashed. What does distort mean here? | To polish something until it shines | To count things carefully | To make a sound louder | *To change the shape so it looks twisted or wrong
exercise: «The news report left out key facts, so it gave a false idea.» Which meaning fits «distort the truth»? | To tell the truth clearly and calmly | To count the days until the news ends | *To change facts so they give a false idea | To make the screen shine brightly
exercise: A guitar player turns on distortion to make the sound rough and crackly. What is distortion? | *A change to a sound that makes it unclear or crackly | A soft, gentle hum | A loud drum beat | A new way to tune a piano
test: A guitar player uses distortion on purpose to make the sound rough and crackly. What does distortion mean? | A new way to tune a piano | A loud drum beat | *A change to a sound so it is unclear or crackly | A soft, gentle hum
test: «The advert tried to distort the facts about the cereal.» What does the advert do? | Shows the cereal in a bright photo | Counts how many boxes were sold | *Changes the facts to give people a false idea | Tells every fact clearly and carefully
exam: «Every flat map distorts something.» What does distorts mean here? | Makes something shine brightly | Adds colour to the edges | Folds a page in half | *Changes the shape or size of something, so it is not exact
